Right before the bomb comes off, it feeds all the systems to it.

52
00:02:59,301 --> 00:03:01,360
Sir, officer, 45-hawks!

53
00:03:01,361 --> 00:03:03,440
The pride that comes with wearing the uniform

54
00:03:03,441 --> 00:03:04,520
never seems to change.

55
00:03:04,521 --> 00:03:06,080
First, skateboarding, not far!

56
00:03:06,081 --> 00:03:07,920
Every day I'm up here, it's always a good experience

57
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:09,920
to come up here and know that I'm doing something

58
00:03:09,921 --> 00:03:12,220
to protect my country and help my shipmates out whenever I can.

59
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:13,680
It's not only one jet that I'm loading.

60
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:15,360
I've got other jets around this jet.

61
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:16,980
If something happens, if something's going wrong,

62
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,280
somebody can die, and I can't have that.

63
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:22,520
The weapons of war, however, are ever-changing.

64
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:25,720
This ordnance team has just loaded a bomb

65
00:03:25,721 --> 00:03:28,980
that can see through clouds and strike within yards of its target,

66
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:32,680
a bomb that 40 years ago was the stuff of science fiction.

67
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:42,520
Until the 1960s, all aerial bombs were gravity-driven.

68
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:46,020
They simply hit wherever gravity and wind currents carried them.

69
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:51,060
They were rarely accurate, killed indiscriminately,

70
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:54,200
and required multiple strikes to achieve success.

71
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:02,840
But the modern battlefield is dominated by smaller,

72
00:04:02,841 --> 00:04:07,720
more accurate smart bombs designed to surgically eliminate military targets.

73
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:15,600
We don't have to drop something down that'll take out an entire city block

74
00:04:15,601 --> 00:04:18,360
because we don't know which part of the block it's going to land in.

75
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:21,500
Now we can put it in a certain building to now we get to the point

76
00:04:21,501 --> 00:04:24,500
where we can put it in the third-story window of a certain building.

77
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:29,700
The Iraq Wars of 1991 and 2003

78
00:04:29,701 --> 00:04:34,020
demonstrated how bombs have evolved into precise, sophisticated weapons.

79
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:37,400
During Operation Desert Storm,

80
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:41,400
the U.S. used 7,400 tons of guided munitions.

81
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,620
In Operation Iraqi Freedom, more than 10,000 tons.

82
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:55,220
Unlike missiles, smart bombs cannot propel themselves.

83
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,280
But the advanced hardware they pack enables them to guide a path

84
00:04:59,281 --> 00:05:00,320
with stunning accuracy.

85
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:06,460
Smart bombs rely on smart people.

86
00:05:06,461 --> 00:05:09,460
A laser-guided bomb depends on either a second aircraft

87
00:05:09,461 --> 00:05:12,460
or a soldier on the ground, getting close enough to the target

88
00:05:12,461 --> 00:05:14,700
to mark it with a laser.

89
00:05:14,701 --> 00:05:17,200
Since the beam reveals the position of those transmitting

90
00:05:17,201 --> 00:05:21,000
it, the laser is aimed at the last possible moment.

91
00:05:21,001 --> 00:05:26,680
The bomb, already in flight, then zeroes in on the mark.

92
00:05:26,681 --> 00:05:30,920
You release the bomb before you put the spot on the target.

93
00:05:30,921 --> 00:05:33,280
You put the spot on the ground or over on a wall

94
00:05:33,281 --> 00:05:35,460
or over on a tree, and then you move it,

95
00:05:35,461 --> 00:05:39,300
because it takes a finite amount of time for that weapon

96
00:05:39,301 --> 00:05:40,720
to get to the tank.

97
00:05:40,721 --> 00:05:43,700
And the tanks sometimes turn around and shoot the laser

98
00:05:43,701 --> 00:05:46,460
operator, and you don't like that.

99
00:05:46,461 --> 00:05:49,780
Another type of smart bomb, a TV-guided weapon,

100
00:05:49,781 --> 00:05:51,880
is steered remotely by a bombardier

101
00:05:51,881 --> 00:05:55,940
operating video game-like controls.

102
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:58,240
A TV-guided weapon, when you saw it,

103
00:05:58,520 --> 00:05:59,780
as it got closer to the target,

104
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:01,420
the target got bigger and bigger and bigger

105
00:06:01,421 --> 00:06:04,360
until it flashed out because you're seeing the camera

106
00:06:04,361 --> 00:06:06,100
moving closer and closer to the target.

107
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:11,180
Although 1990s-era smart bombs struck with great precision,

108
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:14,140
they relied on visual identification of targets.

109
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:18,320
Cloud cover or smoke from fires could obscure targets

110
00:06:18,321 --> 00:06:20,660
and force the cancellation of bombing missions.

111
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:22,600
No enjoy on the target.

112
00:06:22,601 --> 00:06:25,320
I say again, I can't see from the target.

113
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:28,980
In 1991, immediately after Desert Storm,

114
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:31,340
engineers began seeking a solution.

115
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:35,120
They knew the US needed an all-weather smart bomb.

116
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:40,260
It needed JDAM, Joint Direct Attack Munitions.

117
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:43,160
A JDAM has been used in combat in Kosovo,

118
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:47,660
Afghanistan, and Iraq, and something on the order of 15,000

119
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:51,040
JDAMs have been dropped in combat.

120
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:54,160
JDAM converts conventional gravity-driven bombs

121
00:06:54,161 --> 00:06:56,660
bombs into smart ones.

122
00:06:56,661 --> 00:06:59,660
A tail kit containing a global positioning system

123
00:06:59,661 --> 00:07:03,160
and inertial navigation system monitors is attached to

124
00:07:03,161 --> 00:07:10,160
existing 500, 1,000 or 2,000-pound freefall bombs.

125
00:07:10,161 --> 00:07:11,660
This is a JDAM guidance kit.

126
00:07:11,661 --> 00:07:15,660
Right now it's attached to a 2,000-pound Mark 84 warhead.

127
00:07:15,661 --> 00:07:19,160
In just about this section here is the heart or brains of the

128
00:07:19,161 --> 00:07:21,160
navigation and guidance function.

129
00:07:21,161 --> 00:07:23,160
I brought along a unit here.

130
00:07:23,161 --> 00:07:24,740
This is the mission computer.

131
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:29,660
On this side is an inertial measurement unit that has the sensors from the INS,

132
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:32,440
and in the middle is a GPS receiver card.

133
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:39,100
JDAM's navigation monitors send signals to movable tail fins that maintain the bomb's flight path.

134
00:07:40,860 --> 00:07:47,540
It consistently strikes within three meters of its target and operates under all atmospheric conditions.

135
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:51,820
The JDAM weapon is useful in all weather.

136
00:07:51,821 --> 00:07:54,140
So clouds, smoke, it doesn't make any difference.

137
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:56,480
You've launched it from an altitude, you've told it where to go,

138
00:07:56,980 --> 00:07:58,820
it will go to that point on the Earth.

139
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:04,280
G-DAM was originally designed to take out fixed, high-value targets.

140
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:07,620
But its astonishing accuracy made it a powerful tool

141
00:08:07,621 --> 00:08:10,340
against mobile targets deployed near American troops.

142
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:15,680
In Afghanistan, some new things happened.

143
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:17,660
Special Forces troops were put on the ground,

144
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:19,980
they were provided with equipment that would allow them

145
00:08:19,981 --> 00:08:22,580
to actually get coordinates of targets,

146
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:25,380
and they were able to call in JDAMs on targets

147
00:08:25,381 --> 00:08:29,220
that they were actually seeing there that were stationary

148
00:08:29,221 --> 00:08:31,100
but maybe had moved very recently.

149
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:35,780
This is a close air support role

150
00:08:35,781 --> 00:08:38,300
rather than what was originally thought of

151
00:08:38,301 --> 00:08:40,540
as a high-value target attack mode.

152
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:44,880
In years to come, JDAM will be supplemented

153
00:08:44,881 --> 00:08:47,180
by an even smaller and smarter bomb.

154
00:08:47,181 --> 00:08:54,180
A small diameter bomb is the next step in the evolution of miniaturizing weapons.

155
00:08:54,181 --> 00:09:00,180
A small diameter bomb is a 250-pound weapon, but with about 35 pounds of explosives in it.

156
00:09:00,181 --> 00:09:05,180
It has diamondback wing that gives it the ability to fly.

157
00:09:05,181 --> 00:09:11,180
This is a small diameter bomb, mock-up. It's about six feet long, about 250 pounds.

158
00:09:11,181 --> 00:09:18,280
This is the configuration that it would be in when it's stowed on the aircraft, so that it's sleek and aerodynamic.

159
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:20,980
The wings are folded in. The tails are folded in.

160
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:27,880
Once it's launched and dropped off the airplane, the wings will extend, open up like this.

161
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:31,400
The tails will be popping out also.

162
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:38,460
From there on, it will glide itself for 40 or 40-plus miles until it impacts the target.

163
00:09:38,461 --> 00:09:43,400
Small diameter bombs enable planes to carry more weapons

164
00:09:43,401 --> 00:09:45,780
and pilots to fly fewer sorties.

165
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,680
Smaller bombs also create opportunities

166
00:09:48,681 --> 00:09:51,140
to strike multiple targets during a single mission.

167
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:57,060
Now we'll take up a B-1 with 96 small diameter bombs in it

168
00:09:57,061 --> 00:09:59,520
and hit 96 different targets

169
00:09:59,521 --> 00:10:04,620
and hit them precisely and with very, very minimal collateral damage.

170
00:10:04,621 --> 00:10:11,020
Another type of bomb, known as the sensor-fused weapon, or SFW,

171
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:14,180
unleashes many warheads towards multiple targets.

172
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:18,820
Once the SFW is released from the aircraft,

173
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:22,180
gas bags that resemble car airbags inflate

174
00:10:22,181 --> 00:10:25,120
and push out ten separate submunitions.

175
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:29,200
Parachutes then deploy from each submunition,

176
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:33,820
slowing their fall and bringing them into a vertical descent over the target area.

177
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:37,920
At a predetermined height, the parachutes eject

178
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:41,420
and rocket motors fire, stopping the descent.

179
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:44,400
Once the rockets burn out, each submunition

180
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,000
releases four warheads that scan a target area

181
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:50,100
of about 30 acres.

182
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,440
Once the explosive is detonated,

183
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:56,000
that projectile fired at hypervelocities into the target,

184
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:58,380
and it penetrates the armor plating.

185
00:10:58,480 --> 00:10:59,880
Once through that armor plating,

186
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:02,080
it starts to destroy the individual components

187
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:04,620
below that armor.

188
00:11:04,621 --> 00:11:12,340
SFWs are the smartest of the smart, able to track moving targets and distinguish between

189
00:11:12,341 --> 00:11:15,140
Jeeps and tanks.

190
00:11:15,141 --> 00:11:20,420
Each of the 40 warheads has a laser sensor that searches for changes in height, such

191
00:11:20,421 --> 00:11:23,440
as the distinctive contour of a vehicle.

192
00:11:23,441 --> 00:11:28,340
At the same time, infrared sensors seek heat signatures like those emitted by the hot engine

193
00:11:28,341 --> 00:11:30,500
of a target vehicle.

194
00:11:30,501 --> 00:11:36,740
SFWs played a critical role in Operation Iraqi Freedom.

195
00:11:36,741 --> 00:11:42,720
We had lighter armored marine forces making their initial push into Baghdad,

196
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:46,320
starting to come into the lead elements of the Iraqi Guard

197
00:11:46,321 --> 00:11:51,544
that were positioning themselves to ambush and potentially attack the U.S.

198
00:11:51,545 --> 00:11:53,380
forces entering that area.

199
00:11:53,580 --> 00:12:00,100
Called in SFW strikes, and the passes of those weapons absolutely decimated the Iraqi Guard troops

200
00:12:00,101 --> 00:12:03,680
before they were able to effectively employ against the Marine units.

201
00:12:05,180 --> 00:12:07,540
During the height of Operation Iraqi Freedom,

202
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:11,920
SFWs were used 68 times to devastate enemy forces.

203
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:20,380
Sensor-fused weapons are at the cutting edge of so-called data link munitions,

204
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,660
bombs able to identify targets and adjust to attack the highest value kill.

205
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:35,320
Finely tuned image seekers and complex computer algorithms will soon increase the level of information

206
00:12:35,321 --> 00:12:40,960
and may eventually be able to distinguish between hostile and friendly targets.

207
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:48,520
While the first Gulf War accelerated the development of smaller bombs,

208
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:52,960
it also led to the creation of the largest non-nuclear device ever made,

209
00:12:52,961 --> 00:12:55,240
the mother of all bombs.

210
00:12:55,241 --> 00:13:02,980
The word bomb derives from the Latin bombus, meaning a booming sound.

211
00:13:04,060 --> 00:13:06,400
Bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

212
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:18,820
March 11, 2003 marked a milestone in military history.

213
00:13:19,940 --> 00:13:24,620
At Eglin Air Force Base in Florida, the United States tested a new superweapon.

214
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:28,440
MOAB, short for Massive Ordnance Airburst,

215
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:31,540
quickly became known as the mother of all bombs.

216
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:38,280
This massive weapon creates a blast radius stretching

217
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:40,480
a mile in each direction.

218
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:43,020
The MOAB is made out of an aluminum skin,

219
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:45,360
a very thin aluminum skin.

220
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:47,460
And the reason we did it that way was in order

221
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:49,800
to maximize the blast effect.

222
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:52,240
We did not want the airframe of the bomb

223
00:13:52,241 --> 00:13:57,960
to interfere with the development of a blast wave that has the impact to the target.

224
00:13:58,920 --> 00:14:06,440
The world's largest non-nuclear weapon, Moab is over 30 feet long and weighs more than 21,000 pounds.

225
00:14:07,720 --> 00:14:12,800
It's no easy task to accurately maneuver such a behemoth through the skies toward its target.

226
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:20,940
Engineers overcame the challenge with a grid fin design that provides great aerodynamic lift.

227
00:14:20,941 --> 00:14:27,480
During carriage, these four grid fins are folded forward onto the bomb in order that

228
00:14:27,481 --> 00:14:31,720
it makes a very compact design for carriage.

229
00:14:31,721 --> 00:14:39,160
When the weapon is deployed out the back end of the C-130, the fins are subsequently deployed

230
00:14:39,161 --> 00:14:45,460
with aerodynamic assisting in the deployment because the wind is coming from the forward

231
00:14:45,461 --> 00:14:46,960
of the bomb.

232
00:14:46,961 --> 00:14:51,820
Once it's in its flight configuration, as it is shown here, it is then able to control

233
00:14:51,821 --> 00:14:55,280
the bomb and fly it to its designated target.

234
00:14:55,281 --> 00:15:02,240
Although Moab was fast-tracked for Operation Iraqi Freedom, the lack of enemy resistance

235
00:15:02,241 --> 00:15:05,640
kept it grounded.

236
00:15:05,641 --> 00:15:10,440
But its successful test runs served chilling notice of a powerful new force.

237
00:15:10,441 --> 00:15:16,680
The largest guided bomb in the history of the world, with the tremendous impact and

238
00:15:16,681 --> 00:15:20,860
The detonation of this explosive really just shook the area,

239
00:15:20,861 --> 00:15:25,020
and the shockwave could be heard for miles and miles.

240
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:29,020
The deadly potential of bombs such as Moab

241
00:15:29,021 --> 00:15:31,940
is the result of hundreds of years of evolving technology.

242
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:41,100
In the 7th century came the invention of Greek fire,

243
00:15:41,780 --> 00:15:45,060
a kind of primitive napalm used mainly in ship-to-ship combat.

244
00:15:45,061 --> 00:15:48,180
It was a terror weapon of its day.

245
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:52,000
The formula was either never written down or was lost.

246
00:15:52,260 --> 00:15:55,240
It was regarded as a great military secret of the time,

247
00:15:55,960 --> 00:15:58,080
no much as nuclear technology is today.

248
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:00,820
In 12th century Europe,

249
00:16:01,100 --> 00:16:03,680
a new substance emerged with one great advantage.

250
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:04,920
It exploded.

251
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:08,840
Black powder, what we know as gunpowder,

252
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:11,420
was believed to have such mysterious powers

253
00:16:11,421 --> 00:16:13,280
that some deemed it a tool of the devil.

254
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:18,060
Its mixture of sulfur, charcoal, and saltpeter

255
00:16:18,061 --> 00:16:20,140
produced the first true explosive.

256
00:16:22,540 --> 00:16:25,540
Of course, the first cannon came along at the time, too,

257
00:16:25,620 --> 00:16:27,500
and they would fire a simple iron ball.

258
00:16:28,060 --> 00:16:30,340
But sooner or later came the idea,

259
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:32,780
why can't we hollow out the cannonball

260
00:16:32,781 --> 00:16:34,280
and use it to make a bomb?

261
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:35,160
And so they did.

262
00:16:36,380 --> 00:16:39,180
In 16th, as in 20th century warfare,

263
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:41,740
precision was more important than brute force.

264
00:16:42,940 --> 00:16:44,400
Cannonballs gave way to smaller,

265
00:16:44,401 --> 00:16:46,640
yet still powerful hand grenades.

266
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:50,520
The term grenade actually comes from the Spanish granada,

267
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:52,640
which means pomegranate, which a grenade looks like.

268
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:54,580
If you saw a grenade of the period,

269
00:16:54,740 --> 00:16:57,460
say of the 16th or 17th century,

270
00:16:57,520 --> 00:17:00,960
it looks very much like the bombs you see on the cartoon shows.

271
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:03,880
It'd be a round ball with a fuse sticking out of the top.

272
00:17:05,660 --> 00:17:08,920
For grenadiers, bravery was the first order of business.

273
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:12,000
They carried up to a dozen highly volatile

274
00:17:12,001 --> 00:17:15,180
three-pound iron grenades right to the front lines.

275
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:19,960
Then they lit the fuses and threw the grenades at the last moment

276
00:17:19,961 --> 00:17:21,320
before quickly retreating.

277
00:17:22,260 --> 00:17:25,460
Grenadeers are famous because they were brave and they were big.

278
00:17:26,200 --> 00:17:28,820
And they were the real shock troops of the day.

279
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:32,360
Grenades used in the American Civil War

280
00:17:32,361 --> 00:17:34,700
were detonated by percussion caps,

281
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:37,400
a recent invention that eliminated the reliance

282
00:17:37,401 --> 00:17:40,600
on an exposed, delicate flame trailing down a fuse.

283
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:47,000
The percussion system involved a thin metal cap containing black powder,

284
00:17:47,001 --> 00:17:50,000
or a similar substance that exploded on impact.

285
00:17:50,001 --> 00:17:55,000
When detonated, a spark shot into the main charge, igniting it.

286
00:17:55,001 --> 00:18:02,000
In 1846, the Italian chemist Ascanio Sobrero invented nitroglycerin,

287
00:18:02,001 --> 00:18:08,000
a highly volatile mixture of sulfuric and nitric acids and glycerin.

288
00:18:08,001 --> 00:18:11,240
acids, and glycerin.

289
00:18:11,241 --> 00:18:13,580
Simply tapping a jar of nitroglycerin

290
00:18:13,581 --> 00:18:17,380
was enough to trigger an explosion.

291
00:18:17,381 --> 00:18:20,680
They say it'll go off if you look at it cross-eyed.

292
00:18:20,681 --> 00:18:24,860
But it was Albert Nobel, around 1866,

293
00:18:24,861 --> 00:18:27,660
came up with the concept of taking this dreadfully

294
00:18:27,661 --> 00:18:31,920
dangerous liquid and infusing it through clay, basically

295
00:18:31,921 --> 00:18:35,000
making a solid that could then be packed into a cylinder

296
00:18:35,001 --> 00:18:38,520
into a cardboard sheath and used as an explosive.

297
00:18:40,120 --> 00:18:42,260
By stabilizing the nitroglycerin,

298
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:45,840
Nobel had created a far more controllable substance,

299
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:47,220
dynamite.

300
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:49,600
He then modified the percussion cap

301
00:18:49,601 --> 00:18:52,180
so that only an electrical charge would ignite it.

302
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:54,220
You can take a stick of dynamite

303
00:18:54,221 --> 00:18:55,900
and you could whack it on the table

304
00:18:55,901 --> 00:18:59,600
or drive a nail into it or anything of that nature,

305
00:18:59,760 --> 00:19:01,340
and chances are it would not go off.

306
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:04,680
TNT is very similar to dynamite.

307
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:06,480
but it's also malleable,

308
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:09,640
enabling it to be molded into bombs of any shape.

309
00:19:14,140 --> 00:19:17,040
TNT was developed at the dawn of the 20th century,

310
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:20,280
just in time for it to be used

311
00:19:20,281 --> 00:19:23,400
in the bloodiest conflict yet seen in human history.

312
00:19:26,420 --> 00:19:30,080
In 1242, the English scientist Roger Bacon

313
00:19:30,081 --> 00:19:32,600
wrote the first known recipe for gunpowder.

314
00:19:32,601 --> 00:19:35,360
He was so frightened by the danger of this mixture

315
00:19:35,361 --> 00:19:39,180
that he disguised the recipe in a nearly indecipherable anagram.

316
00:19:40,260 --> 00:19:42,720
Bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

317
00:19:49,880 --> 00:19:53,120
Modern bombing missions are highly complex operations,

318
00:19:53,820 --> 00:19:55,300
planned to the last detail.

319
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:57,560
Going out the door, walking to your jet,

320
00:19:57,680 --> 00:19:59,120
you know what target you're going for.

321
00:19:59,121 --> 00:20:01,740
As soon as you get into range of that target,

322
00:20:01,741 --> 00:20:03,860
you're dropping automatically.

323
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:09,400
High-tech bombs help pilots distance themselves from harm's way.

324
00:20:10,020 --> 00:20:12,800
The more complex and more accurate we can get our weapons,

325
00:20:13,060 --> 00:20:14,900
the better it makes me feel as a pilot.

326
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:17,960
Being able to stand off from the target and release a weapon

327
00:20:17,961 --> 00:20:19,880
is a comforting feeling.

328
00:20:23,720 --> 00:20:26,720
History's first aerial bombers were right in the thick of the action.

329
00:20:28,120 --> 00:20:30,940
After the advent of airplanes early in the 20th century,

330
00:20:31,540 --> 00:20:33,720
military leaders adapted them for combat

331
00:20:33,721 --> 00:20:35,620
and changed warfare forever.

332
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:38,300
There weren't any bomb sites or bomb racks,

333
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:40,600
but you could take an artillery shell

334
00:20:40,601 --> 00:20:42,240
and have fins fabricated for it

335
00:20:42,241 --> 00:20:44,820
and drop it over the side just by hand

336
00:20:44,821 --> 00:20:47,040
using an eyeball to estimate it

337
00:20:47,041 --> 00:20:49,040
and maybe hurt somebody by chance.

338
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:52,060
And so each side did that.

339
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:55,340
Aviation technology progressed rapidly.

340
00:20:56,340 --> 00:20:59,120
By 1918, single-engine biplanes

341
00:20:59,121 --> 00:21:01,240
have been replaced by multi-engine aircraft,

342
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:04,040
capable of carrying 1,000 pounds of bombs.

343
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,440
As airplanes get larger and have more powerful engines,

344
00:21:08,820 --> 00:21:12,120
can lift more payload, they can carry bigger bombs.

345
00:21:12,121 --> 00:21:15,200
We see the concept of an air-powered doctrine

346
00:21:15,201 --> 00:21:18,240
being developed of masses of airplanes,

347
00:21:18,800 --> 00:21:21,000
of fighters escorting bombers,

348
00:21:21,740 --> 00:21:24,320
of reconnaissance or spy aircraft.

349
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:28,380
So much of what we see over the next 60 years

350
00:21:28,381 --> 00:21:31,560
in aviation had its birth in World War I.

351
00:21:34,640 --> 00:21:37,700
Two decades after the war to end all wars,

352
00:21:38,380 --> 00:21:41,800
World War II sparked a massive escalation in aerial bombardment.

353
00:21:42,620 --> 00:21:45,880
Huge raids, first by the Germans and then by the Allies,

354
00:21:46,360 --> 00:21:49,380
demonstrated the destructive power of the conventional aerial bomb.

355
00:21:55,460 --> 00:21:58,360
The most devastating raids used incendiaries,

356
00:21:58,380 --> 00:22:02,740
bombs filled with gasoline that burst into flames on impact.

357
00:22:05,560 --> 00:22:07,880
On a March night in 1945,

358
00:22:08,880 --> 00:22:12,940
swarms of Allied bombers turned Tokyo into a raging fireball.

359
00:22:15,660 --> 00:22:18,400
The first wave of planes dropped high explosives

360
00:22:18,401 --> 00:22:22,020
to blow the roofs off buildings, exposing vulnerable timber.

361
00:22:23,360 --> 00:22:26,180
That wood was then ignited by hundreds of planes

362
00:22:26,181 --> 00:22:28,020
dropping thousands of incendiaries.

363
00:22:28,380 --> 00:22:37,320
Temperatures soared above 2,700 degrees Fahrenheit.

364
00:22:38,100 --> 00:22:42,200
As hot air rose rapidly, cold air rushed in from the outside,

365
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:44,340
sucking people into the firestorm.

366
00:22:46,560 --> 00:22:48,880
Certainly more people were killed in that attack

367
00:22:48,881 --> 00:22:50,820
than were in the first atomic bomb attack.

368
00:22:52,420 --> 00:22:55,020
World War II gave rise to carpet bombing,

369
00:22:55,820 --> 00:22:58,200
blanketing a vast area with thousands of bombs

370
00:22:58,201 --> 00:22:59,400
that might land anywhere.

371
00:23:00,760 --> 00:23:02,880
The most sighting equipment for bombardiers

372
00:23:02,881 --> 00:23:04,080
was crude and ineffective.

373
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:07,340
Tacticians demanded a better method.

374
00:23:08,620 --> 00:23:10,640
The whole science that we're talking about

375
00:23:10,641 --> 00:23:13,360
is not that the bomb is such a wonderful device.

376
00:23:14,080 --> 00:23:16,700
It's putting it exactly where you want it.

377
00:23:17,440 --> 00:23:19,240
In World War II, you could deliver them

378
00:23:19,241 --> 00:23:20,240
with much more precision.

379
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:24,220
First with the famous American Norton bombsite,

380
00:23:24,221 --> 00:23:26,360
which could drop a bomb in a pickle barrel

381
00:23:26,361 --> 00:23:31,100
from 25,000 feet, which, given ideal conditions, it could.

382
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:37,640
The Norton bombsite was one of the first mechanical computers.

383
00:23:38,600 --> 00:23:42,160
A bombardier adjusted knobs and levers to set a series of miniature gears.

384
00:23:43,320 --> 00:23:45,240
He keyed in several variables.

385
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:49,060
The exact flight path, the plane's airspeed,

386
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:54,140
latest updates on crosswinds, distance to the target.

387
00:23:54,141 --> 00:23:58,720
Finally, the bombardier made one last sighting on the target

388
00:23:58,721 --> 00:24:00,440
and activated the bombsite.

389
00:24:01,520 --> 00:24:03,960
The Norton then took control of the airplane,

390
00:24:04,540 --> 00:24:06,080
guiding it during the final moments

391
00:24:06,081 --> 00:24:08,240
and automatically releasing the payload.

392
00:24:09,980 --> 00:24:12,160
The Norton bombsite was a big improvement

393
00:24:12,161 --> 00:24:14,460
over anything else that existed in those days.

394
00:24:14,980 --> 00:24:18,960
In fact, hardly anything more than an X on a canopy

395
00:24:18,961 --> 00:24:20,900
was available before that.

396
00:24:20,901 --> 00:24:27,040
Hitler's Third Reich pioneered other frontiers in bomb development.

397
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:32,700
Their method for propelling bombs was rather simple in theory.

398
00:24:33,420 --> 00:24:34,640
Attach them to jet engines.

399
00:24:35,700 --> 00:24:39,760
Hitler named his new guided missile the V-1 for Vengeance One.

400
00:24:39,761 --> 00:24:46,040
The harbinger of modern missile warfare were the German V-1 guided missiles.

401
00:24:46,041 --> 00:24:50,840
It was a very simple weapon, traveled a couple of hundred miles.

402
00:24:51,580 --> 00:24:56,900
Its fuel would cut off at a certain point, and it would just nose over, hit the target.

403
00:24:57,800 --> 00:24:59,360
Accuracy was very poor.

404
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:09,680
The V-1 program, however, was just part of Germany's plan for ultimate victory.

405
00:25:09,681 --> 00:25:18,380
In 1938, German scientists fueled Hitler's dream of world conquest when they discovered

406
00:25:18,381 --> 00:25:19,180
nuclear fission.

407
00:25:20,180 --> 00:25:23,780
Very quickly, that information spread throughout the scientific community.

408
00:25:24,080 --> 00:25:30,080
The moment that information spread, it was immediately realized that this discovery could

409
00:25:30,081 --> 00:25:33,420
be used to make a great source of energy or a bomb.

410
00:25:33,421 --> 00:25:37,900
By 1942, the threat of Germany producing a nuclear weapon

411
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,360
compelled American leaders to take bold action.

412
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:47,800
The race was on to create the world's first atomic bomb.

413
00:25:50,640 --> 00:25:53,540
In addition to Hitler's V-1 jet propelled bomb,

414
00:25:53,640 --> 00:25:56,540
Germany produced the more powerful V-2 model.

415
00:25:56,640 --> 00:25:58,820
It was the world's first ballistic missile

416
00:25:58,920 --> 00:26:02,240
and had a range of up to 250 miles.

417
00:26:02,241 --> 00:26:05,240
Bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

418
00:26:08,240 --> 00:26:11,240
Today, the awesome destructive power of nuclear weapons

419
00:26:11,241 --> 00:26:13,240
is a basic fact of life.

420
00:26:16,240 --> 00:26:18,240
The knowledge needed to create atomic bombs

421
00:26:18,241 --> 00:26:20,240
is widely accessible.

422
00:26:20,241 --> 00:26:22,240
Only the scarcity of nuclear materials

423
00:26:22,241 --> 00:26:23,240
limits their proliferation.

424
00:26:26,240 --> 00:26:29,240
In the 1930s, only a handful of scientists

425
00:26:29,241 --> 00:26:31,240
envisioned the possibilities of nuclear weapons.

426
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:38,860
Nuclear fission requires an extremely dense material, such as uranium or plutonium, which

427
00:26:38,861 --> 00:26:41,300
becomes the core of the bomb.

428
00:26:41,301 --> 00:26:48,140
The atomic bomb goes off because you have a mass of plutonium struck by a neutron that

429
00:26:48,141 --> 00:26:54,840
gives you fission that produces more neutrons, that hits more plutonium, that gives you more

430
00:26:54,841 --> 00:26:59,200
fission, releasing more neutrons.

431
00:26:59,201 --> 00:27:00,900
what they call a chain reaction.

432
00:27:01,340 --> 00:27:04,480
And it just goes on until the whole thing blows apart.

433
00:27:04,840 --> 00:27:07,180
It takes less than a millionth of a second.

434
00:27:10,440 --> 00:27:13,200
Hitler held an early lead in the atomic race

435
00:27:13,201 --> 00:27:15,280
destined to redefine the course of history.

436
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:17,480
But he squandered his advantage

437
00:27:17,481 --> 00:27:19,480
by threatening his best scientists.

438
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:23,980
The problem was so many of the German scientists were Jewish.

439
00:27:24,780 --> 00:27:28,120
And Hitler had no use for Jewish science or Jewish scientists.

440
00:27:28,121 --> 00:27:32,200
and so many of them escaped, came to the United States,

441
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:37,480
carried on the idea, and eventually they realized

442
00:27:37,481 --> 00:27:40,360
that this could lead to practical results

443
00:27:40,361 --> 00:27:43,600
as a controlled explosion, a bomb.

444
00:27:45,100 --> 00:27:47,500
Scientists, including the great Albert Einstein,

445
00:27:48,200 --> 00:27:50,540
worried that Germany might still become the first nation

446
00:27:50,541 --> 00:27:51,960
to produce an atomic bomb.

447
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:56,620
Hungarian refugee physicists in the United States,

448
00:27:56,621 --> 00:28:02,500
Leo Szilard and Edward Teller encouraged Einstein to write a letter to President Franklin Roosevelt,

449
00:28:03,400 --> 00:28:05,760
urging him to speed development of a nuclear weapon.

450
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:10,520
They knew they needed Einstein's clout to get the president's ear.

451
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:30,120
everything in the world, except he did not know how to drive a car.

452
00:28:31,240 --> 00:28:33,780
So he asked me to drive him to Einstein.

453
00:28:34,780 --> 00:28:39,840
When Einstein read what he but Szilard has written,

454
00:28:40,780 --> 00:28:45,420
I asked a few questions about it and then said yes, yes, and signed it.

455
00:28:47,020 --> 00:28:50,920
Einstein's urging and the looming threat of an atomic bomb in the hands of the Nazis

456
00:28:50,921 --> 00:28:53,100
compelled Roosevelt to take action.

457
00:28:53,101 --> 00:28:57,900
In late 1942, he authorized the Manhattan Project,

458
00:28:57,901 --> 00:29:01,280
an all-out effort to build the world's first atomic bomb.

459
00:29:01,980 --> 00:29:05,440
It soon became one of the most ambitious undertakings in American history.

460
00:29:06,600 --> 00:29:11,000
It was 150,000 people, $20 billion in today's money,

461
00:29:11,220 --> 00:29:13,440
and it literally spread from sea to sea,

462
00:29:13,740 --> 00:29:16,220
from Hanford, where they were making plutonium,

463
00:29:16,380 --> 00:29:20,420
back to Oak Ridge, where they were making enriched uranium and U-235.

464
00:29:21,120 --> 00:29:25,500
Every major university, every major corporation in America was involved.

465
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:31,080
Los Alamos, New Mexico was chosen as the headquarters of the project.

466
00:29:32,140 --> 00:29:36,760
Far from the coasts, a tiny city could be neither observed nor bombed from the air.

467
00:29:37,560 --> 00:29:39,820
It's climate-permitted outdoor work year-round.

468
00:29:41,800 --> 00:29:45,320
It also had a rail line to transport personnel and supplies,

469
00:29:45,321 --> 00:29:48,560
as well as a complex of school buildings

470
00:29:48,561 --> 00:29:51,260
that could be converted to engineering labs.

471
00:29:52,200 --> 00:29:54,700
As secret as the Manhattan Project was,

472
00:29:55,060 --> 00:29:57,840
the kids at the Los Alamos Ranch School were no dummies.

473
00:29:58,500 --> 00:30:00,640
When two special visitors arrived,

474
00:30:00,960 --> 00:30:03,180
the students recognized them instantly.

475
00:30:03,940 --> 00:30:05,840
They were international physicists,

476
00:30:06,640 --> 00:30:09,420
nuclear physicists that they had seen in their textbooks.

477
00:30:09,840 --> 00:30:12,640
So the kids knew something big was going to happen,

478
00:30:12,641 --> 00:30:14,940
and it was going to involve nuclear physics.

479
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:19,500
For the next 27 months, scientists raced the clock

480
00:30:19,501 --> 00:30:21,500
to create the world's first atomic bomb.

481
00:30:22,620 --> 00:30:26,460
They toiled day and night, squeezing a decade's worth of advances

482
00:30:26,461 --> 00:30:29,520
in physics and engineering into a handful of months.

483
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:35,960
In the summer of 1945, Manhattan Project scientists

484
00:30:35,961 --> 00:30:37,800
put the finishing touches on the bomb.

485
00:30:38,940 --> 00:30:43,620
On July 16th, in southern New Mexico, engineers prepared to test it.

486
00:30:43,621 --> 00:30:48,620
code-named the gadget, it was hoisted atop a huge tower.

487
00:30:48,621 --> 00:30:55,620
Some scientists worried that the chain reaction they hoped to trigger would fail to occur.

488
00:30:55,621 --> 00:31:02,620
Others feared the opposite, that the explosion would prove so powerful it would set the atmosphere on fire.

489
00:31:05,620 --> 00:31:10,620
A terrific storm, an electrical storm, started early in the morning,

490
00:31:10,621 --> 00:31:14,760
and the fear was it would spontaneously somehow detonate the device.

491
00:31:15,700 --> 00:31:18,200
The weather cleared just around 5.30.

492
00:31:18,201 --> 00:31:21,540
They started the countdown and history began.

493
00:31:24,440 --> 00:31:27,880
I was there, 20 miles away from the explosion.

494
00:31:30,020 --> 00:31:33,480
And what I saw was disappointing.

495
00:31:36,840 --> 00:31:39,180
Just a little light on the horizon.

496
00:31:39,181 --> 00:31:41,180
which slightly grew.

497
00:31:41,181 --> 00:31:47,180
And so I tried to look out,

498
00:31:47,181 --> 00:31:52,180
aside from the dark glasses and protection.

499
00:31:52,181 --> 00:31:57,180
And suddenly, as we were in a complete dark room,

500
00:31:57,181 --> 00:32:00,180
the curtains would have been raised.

501
00:32:00,181 --> 00:32:02,180
The light was streaming in.

502
00:32:02,181 --> 00:32:06,180
That was perhaps not quite a minute after the explosion.

503
00:32:06,181 --> 00:32:09,772
the explosion. Then I was impressed.

504
00:32:09,773 --> 00:32:15,060
It became brighter and brighter and rose and we knew,

505
00:32:15,061 --> 00:32:21,220
I knew, that soon it will be used over Japan and then it will not be just an experiment.

506
00:32:21,860 --> 00:32:25,540
After the test, engineers completed the construction of two bombs.

507
00:32:28,020 --> 00:32:30,340
Little Boy contained a uranium core.

508
00:32:30,341 --> 00:32:36,180
and Fat Man held the recently discovered Plutonian.

509
00:32:40,020 --> 00:32:44,360
Harry Truman, who had assumed the presidency when FDR suddenly died,

510
00:32:44,880 --> 00:32:48,060
made the fateful decision that using the bombs would shorten the war

511
00:32:48,061 --> 00:32:50,500
and forestall a bloody invasion of Japan.

512
00:32:53,440 --> 00:32:59,740
On August 6, 1945, the U.S. B-29 bomber Enola Gay

513
00:32:59,741 --> 00:33:02,740
took off with Little Boy for Hiroshima, Japan.

514
00:33:04,880 --> 00:33:08,560
At 8.15 a.m., from an altitude of 31,000 feet,

515
00:33:09,420 --> 00:33:13,120
it jettisoned its payload and raced away from the target area.

516
00:33:13,900 --> 00:33:17,300
The first part of the firing sequence was when wires,

517
00:33:17,480 --> 00:33:21,100
which were attached to the bomb and attached to the plane, pulled out.

518
00:33:21,880 --> 00:33:24,880
These started 15-second clock timer switches,

519
00:33:25,380 --> 00:33:28,800
which then made sure that the plane was completely away,

520
00:33:28,801 --> 00:33:32,100
that it had made this diving turn and was out of the area.

521
00:33:32,800 --> 00:33:39,780
At that point, the barometric switches passed control over to the radar antennae.

522
00:33:39,781 --> 00:33:45,800
These radar antennae detected how far off the ground the bomb actually was.

523
00:33:46,420 --> 00:33:50,620
And at 2,000 feet, they sent a firing signal back,

524
00:33:50,920 --> 00:33:55,240
which started an irreversible set of events leading to the nuclear explosion.

525
00:33:58,800 --> 00:34:04,860
Little Boy exploded with a force of 15 kilotons, or 15,000 tons of TNT.

526
00:34:07,080 --> 00:34:14,280
On August 9, 1945, at 1.02 a.m., the U.S. dropped Fat Man over Nagasaki, Japan.

527
00:34:15,200 --> 00:34:18,240
It exploded with a force of 21 kilotons of TNT.

528
00:34:19,680 --> 00:34:23,820
Temperatures at ground zero rose to approximately 7,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

529
00:34:24,960 --> 00:34:27,800
Wind velocity reached 620 miles per hour.

530
00:34:28,600 --> 00:34:32,280
Blast pressure was 4,600 pounds per square foot.

531
00:34:33,460 --> 00:34:35,180
Survivors who witnessed the explosions

532
00:34:35,181 --> 00:34:38,200
likened them to seeing another sun in the sky.

533
00:34:39,080 --> 00:34:41,600
The intense heat wave that followed the explosions

534
00:34:41,601 --> 00:34:44,840
destroyed all life within a radius of nearly two miles.

535
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:55,460
Little Boy and Fat Man killed an estimated 200,000 people.

536
00:34:57,800 --> 00:35:03,780
As horrible as it is to say, it was a blessing because the Japanese people were able to surrender then.

537
00:35:04,220 --> 00:35:07,600
Their military did not permit them to surrender, wouldn't countenance it,

538
00:35:07,820 --> 00:35:11,280
until finally, after the second bomb was dropped, the emperor insisted upon it.

539
00:35:15,480 --> 00:35:18,860
Four years after Hiroshima, in August 1949,

540
00:35:19,680 --> 00:35:23,760
the Soviet Union shocked the West by detonating its first atomic weapon.

541
00:35:23,761 --> 00:35:31,860
By the end of that year, there were rumors that the Russians were close to producing a fusion, or hydrogen bomb,

542
00:35:32,420 --> 00:35:35,520
said to be a thousand times more powerful than the atomic bomb.

543
00:35:37,980 --> 00:35:42,400
Fusion is the opposite of fission, whereas fission is splitting apart,

544
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:46,900
splitting a very heavy uranium or plutonium nucleus.

545
00:35:47,860 --> 00:35:51,880
With fusion, you're going to the other end of the periodic table of elements,

546
00:35:51,881 --> 00:35:55,380
to the very lightest ones, and you are fusing them

547
00:35:55,381 --> 00:35:59,680
or squeezing them together, squeezing the nuclei together

548
00:35:59,681 --> 00:36:02,080
to make a new compound or a new element.

549
00:36:03,800 --> 00:36:06,720
The superheated material expands violently

550
00:36:06,721 --> 00:36:09,800
to cool itself, generating the enormous shock wave

551
00:36:09,801 --> 00:36:10,940
of a hydrogen blast.

552
00:36:12,480 --> 00:36:17,160
In January 1950, President Truman ordered the United States

553
00:36:17,161 --> 00:36:20,780
to investigate the possibility of producing hydrogen bombs.

554
00:36:21,640 --> 00:36:24,460
Physicist Edward Teller was placed in charge of the program.

555
00:36:25,960 --> 00:36:29,300
I worked on it probably longer than anybody else.

556
00:36:30,480 --> 00:36:34,060
Certainly earlier than anybody else, knowing what we did.

557
00:36:36,120 --> 00:36:38,000
The question arose, shall we now go ahead?

558
00:36:39,840 --> 00:36:46,000
Among the people who had enough access in Washington

559
00:36:46,001 --> 00:36:49,060
and enough reputation to be listened to,

560
00:36:49,061 --> 00:36:51,700
I was the only one who said,

561
00:36:52,500 --> 00:36:54,340
it can be done and it should be done.

562
00:36:55,800 --> 00:36:59,240
Within three years, both the U.S. and Russia

563
00:36:59,241 --> 00:37:00,780
developed hydrogen bombs.

564
00:37:01,940 --> 00:37:04,980
Soon afterwards, both countries adopted the military strategy

565
00:37:04,981 --> 00:37:08,360
called MAG for mutual assured destruction.

566
00:37:09,420 --> 00:37:12,860
Each side recognized that any full-scale use of nuclear weapons

567
00:37:12,861 --> 00:37:14,820
would decimate both nations.

568
00:37:19,060 --> 00:37:21,560
In the quantities in which it was ultimately produced,

569
00:37:21,660 --> 00:37:23,000
it would have ended civilization.

570
00:37:23,100 --> 00:37:25,860
If we'd had a nuclear exchange, as we might have had,

571
00:37:25,960 --> 00:37:29,040
if we hadn't had reasonable leaders on both sides,

572
00:37:29,140 --> 00:37:31,540
the world as we know it could have been like Neville

573
00:37:31,640 --> 00:37:32,600
shoots on the beach.

574
00:37:32,700 --> 00:37:33,880
I mean, everybody might have been dead.

575
00:37:40,680 --> 00:37:44,380
During the Cold War, the overwhelming destructive firepower

576
00:37:44,480 --> 00:37:46,780
possessed by the superpowers actually

577
00:37:46,781 --> 00:37:50,080
actually helped avert a nuclear nightmare.

578
00:37:50,081 --> 00:37:53,580
But today, new terrors and new tactics

579
00:37:53,581 --> 00:37:56,380
have given rise to grave new threats.

580
00:38:01,420 --> 00:38:04,380
One pound of uranium about the size of a golf ball

581
00:38:04,381 --> 00:38:10,380
can produce as much explosive energy as 8,000 tons of TNT.

582
00:38:10,381 --> 00:38:14,380
Bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

583
00:38:16,780 --> 00:38:26,580
In the 21st century, Los Alamos, birthplace of the atomic bomb, continues to lead the

584
00:38:26,581 --> 00:38:29,980
way in American nuclear research.

585
00:38:29,981 --> 00:38:35,820
It's home to a facility called DART, that's short for Dual Axis Radiographic Hydrodynamic

586
00:38:35,821 --> 00:38:36,820
Test Facility.

587
00:38:36,821 --> 00:38:41,780
We want to understand how our weapons are going to work and if they're going to work

588
00:38:41,781 --> 00:38:43,220
reliably.

589
00:38:43,221 --> 00:38:45,100
Are they aging?

590
00:38:45,101 --> 00:38:47,940
Will they work if we ever need them?

591
00:38:47,941 --> 00:38:50,660
Are they safe as they currently are?

592
00:38:50,661 --> 00:38:58,780
DART is one way that we can run very controlled experiments with non-nuclear materials and

593
00:38:58,781 --> 00:39:03,640
understand the data.

594
00:39:03,641 --> 00:39:09,180
Much of the US nuclear stockpile dates from the 1960s and 70s and has long exceeded its

595
00:39:09,181 --> 00:39:14,320
designed shelf life of 15 to 20 years.

596
00:39:14,321 --> 00:39:16,860
these aging warheads still detonate?

597
00:39:16,861 --> 00:39:19,560
Will they remain safe and secure in the event of an accident?

598
00:39:22,460 --> 00:39:25,580
Answering these questions becomes ever more difficult,

599
00:39:25,581 --> 00:39:29,760
because the US no longer tests actual warheads.

600
00:39:29,761 --> 00:39:35,000
International treaties enacted in 1962 and again in 1992

601
00:39:35,001 --> 00:39:37,440
first moved nuclear tests underground

602
00:39:37,441 --> 00:39:39,360
and then banned them entirely.

603
00:39:39,361 --> 00:39:42,480
It's much like trying to maintain a fighter aircraft

604
00:39:42,481 --> 00:39:46,980
without ever being able to turn it on or fly it.

605
00:39:46,981 --> 00:39:48,900
You have to examine all the components

606
00:39:48,901 --> 00:39:53,280
of that piece of equipment, study them very carefully,

607
00:39:53,281 --> 00:39:55,840
put them back together again, and then certify

608
00:39:55,841 --> 00:39:58,400
that if you need to fly that aircraft, it'll work.

609
00:39:58,401 --> 00:40:01,260
And that's what we have to do with nuclear weapons.

610
00:40:03,420 --> 00:40:06,840
DART tests the viability of America's nuclear weapons

611
00:40:06,841 --> 00:40:09,180
by simulating the effects of a nuclear blast

612
00:40:09,181 --> 00:40:11,200
under controlled laboratory conditions.

613
00:40:11,201 --> 00:40:12,200
The radio is charged.

614
00:40:12,201 --> 00:40:13,700
Count begins at 5.

615
00:40:13,701 --> 00:40:19,200
5, 4, 3, 2, 1, fire.

616
00:40:23,200 --> 00:40:25,200
The instant of detonation is captured

617
00:40:25,201 --> 00:40:30,200
by two giant x-ray machines set at a right angle.

618
00:40:30,201 --> 00:40:33,200
They fire extraordinarily brief and intense flashes

619
00:40:33,201 --> 00:40:37,200
of radiation to create a 3-D image.

620
00:40:37,201 --> 00:40:40,200
The actual x-ray images are classified.

621
00:40:40,201 --> 00:40:44,240
These computer simulations depict the type of activity seen during the explosions.

622
00:40:45,760 --> 00:40:50,620
The reason we have to use x-rays to penetrate these experiments

623
00:40:50,621 --> 00:40:57,600
is because they're very dense material, and the explosion is occurring very quickly.

624
00:40:58,420 --> 00:41:04,140
You could never film what's occurring inside the experiment with traditional cameras.

625
00:41:04,141 --> 00:41:08,120
You must penetrate it with x-rays to look through it.

626
00:41:08,121 --> 00:41:12,680
This is the electron beam accelerator that's the heart of the first start x-ray source.

627
00:41:13,240 --> 00:41:16,400
We make electrons and then convert those electrons into x-rays.

628
00:41:16,620 --> 00:41:21,960
The electrons are generated first here and then are accelerated in the electron beam accelerator itself.

629
00:41:22,500 --> 00:41:26,220
Think of the electron beam accelerator like a marble rolling down a staircase.

630
00:41:26,660 --> 00:41:30,520
Every time the marble reaches a stair, it gains energy as it falls down.

631
00:41:30,521 --> 00:41:37,300
Well, our marbles are electrons, and each time it reaches an area of the accelerator shown by one of these cans, it gains energy.

632
00:41:38,120 --> 00:41:40,160
A tremendous amount of power is generated

633
00:41:40,260 --> 00:41:42,900
to fire the high-powered X-ray bursts,

634
00:41:43,000 --> 00:41:47,800
which last less than 1 10,000th of a second.

635
00:41:47,900 --> 00:41:52,740
Each of these power supplies produce 250,000 volts.

636
00:41:52,840 --> 00:41:55,300
When we combine their output,

637
00:41:55,400 --> 00:41:59,580
DART produces a 15 million-volt pulse

638
00:41:59,680 --> 00:42:03,180
for 70 billionths of a second.

639
00:42:03,280 --> 00:42:06,140
As DART continues analyzing nuclear warheads,

640
00:42:06,141 --> 00:42:10,480
Other organizations struggle to defend against a very different type of weapon.

641
00:42:12,020 --> 00:42:19,300
They're the bombs used by terrorists, so-called improvised explosive devices, or IEDs.

642
00:42:19,960 --> 00:42:25,360
These homemade bombs have grabbed headlines and shattered the lives of thousands around the world.

643
00:42:26,060 --> 00:42:30,500
In Oklahoma City, Madrid, and London.

644
00:42:32,000 --> 00:42:33,220
Terrorists are not stupid.

645
00:42:33,221 --> 00:42:38,300
They don't just throw an IED out somewhere and hope somebody runs across it.

646
00:42:38,600 --> 00:42:42,240
Every single IED bomb has a target.

647
00:42:42,780 --> 00:42:49,480
The difference is it's hard for maybe somebody standing back to look at it and figure out what the target is.

648
00:42:50,340 --> 00:42:53,920
IEDs generally use basic materials and simple techniques.

649
00:42:54,720 --> 00:43:03,100
Any device that sends an electronic signal, a garage door opener, a TV remote, or a cell phone can trigger a deadly explosion.

650
00:43:03,220 --> 00:43:07,220
You're really going up against not something conventional,

651
00:43:07,221 --> 00:43:10,220
but somebody else's imagination, somebody else's mind.

652
00:43:10,221 --> 00:43:13,220
What can they put together? What can they come up with?

653
00:43:13,221 --> 00:43:17,220
And how can you be smarter than them and defeat it?

654
00:43:17,221 --> 00:43:21,220
The hardest terrorist to defeat is the one willing to give his life.

655
00:43:21,221 --> 00:43:25,220
What we face today is an extraordinary paradox.

656
00:43:25,221 --> 00:43:30,220
On the one hand, the developed nations have these weapons of enormous destruction,

657
00:43:30,221 --> 00:43:36,220
these megaton-level nuclear weapons capable of destroying a whole city.

658
00:43:36,221 --> 00:43:40,220
On the other hand, in this incredible asymmetry,

659
00:43:40,221 --> 00:43:44,220
we worry about people arriving inside a crowded pizza parlor

660
00:43:44,221 --> 00:43:47,220
with an explosive strapped around their waist.

661
00:43:49,220 --> 00:43:52,220
For decades, the threat of mutual assured destruction

662
00:43:52,221 --> 00:43:56,220
helped the U.S. and the Soviet Union to maintain a fragile peace.

663
00:43:56,221 --> 00:44:02,220
Today, a new global battle pits the world's most advanced weapons

664
00:44:02,221 --> 00:44:05,220
against low-tech terrorist explosives.

665
00:44:05,221 --> 00:44:11,220
The future is uncertain, but it seems clear that bombs will

666
00:44:11,221 --> 00:44:16,220
continue to play a decisive and devastating role in human affairs.

667
00:44:26,220 --> 00:44:36,460
The return of IceRod.

